diagnosis_text:
A complete investigation was conducted covering all four diagnostic phases. Phase 1 found zero packet loss across all measured paths (pc1, pc2, pc3 all mutually reachable at 0% loss). Phase A (infra_sweep / l2_snapshot) revealed ACL rules on dswa1/dswa2 that block 10.1.0.0/24→10.3.0.0/24 with zero matched packets — an inter-VLAN policy that has never fired and is unrelated to the reported symptom. Phase B (ospf_snapshot / tc_snapshot) confirmed the OSPF fabric is healthy with full adjacencies on all distribution-to-core links, and no traffic-shaping qdiscs are present. Phases C/D (dhcp_link_history, pressure_sweep) found no carrier flap history, no stress tools, and no suspicious processes. Direct verification on dswa1 and dswa2 confirmed: keepalived is running on both switches, dswa1 is the stable VRRP master holding 10.1.0.1/32 on br0.10, dswa2 is the clean backup (no VIP), all interface carrier/error counters are at zero, and no keepalived syslog or journal entries recording any VRRP state change exist. The keepalived configuration uses a non-default advert_int of 4 s (vs the 1 s default), which would lengthen failover detection time to approximately 16 s if dswa1 silently disappeared — but this setting does not autonomously cause VRRP flapping. The helpdesk claim that the gateway "keeps dropping out" is unconfirmed: no flap mechanism, no carrier-change trace, no log evidence, and no process capable of inducing it was found. Root cause: there is no active fault. Fix: no corrective action is required; the current VRRP state is stable and functioning correctly.
=== END ===